1079 #uuid "4dc2b09f-c2d7-5d6c-8b4d-48c2f11ae6c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502017 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502017 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502017 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502017 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502017 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502017 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502017 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502017 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502017 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "752081da-3806-5149-83db-ac2d9b472911" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502019 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502019 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502019 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502019 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502019 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502019 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502019 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502019 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502019 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7ae92825-baa7-50ef-a722-e714030d9c79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502100 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502100 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502100 #edb/ref 1071 "A tank initially holds $420\\,\\text{gal}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $14\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt.\nFresh water is pumped into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of salt in the tank $20$ minutes after the fresh water pumping begins." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502100 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4779b0e3-7c68-54eb-98ad-ac554dad498b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502100 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nNote that fresh water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $420\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine; water enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and drains out at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n420+12t-12t=420\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{35}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{Q}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{1}{35}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{35} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{35})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{1}{35}\\int 1\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{t}{35}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-\\frac{t}{35}+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-t/35},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $14\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=14,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =14 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/35} & =14 \\\\ A & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=14{e}^{-t/35}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the tank $20$ minutes after the fresh water is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(20)=14{e}^{-20/35}≈7.91\\,\\text{lb},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502115 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502115 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336622" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502115 #edb/ref 1071 "A tank initially holds $280\\,\\text{L}$ of a chemical solution containing $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved chemical.\nA chemical solution containing $0.20\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved chemical per liter is poured into the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of dissolved chemical $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the solution begins to be added?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502115 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "787955c7-f519-56e2-a7ff-67abb164ab3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502115 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of dissolved chemical in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dissolved chemical enters the tank minus the rate at which dissolved chemical leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved chemical enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a chemical solution containing $0.20\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved chemical enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dissolved chemical* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.20\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.20\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =3.2\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved chemical exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which dissolved chemical leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $280\\,\\text{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Chemical solution enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $16t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $16t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n280+16t-16t=280\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of dissolved chemical in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dissolved chemical* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{35}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =3.2-\\frac{2Q}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{35}Q=3.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502122 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502122 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336551" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502122 #edb/ref 1071 "A reactor initially holds $520\\,\\text{L}$ of a chemical solution containing $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of reactant.\nA pure solvent is poured into the reactor at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the reactor at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of reactant $Q,$ in kilograms, in the reactor $t$ minutes after the solvent begins to be added?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502122 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ce0dbae-abd1-5d37-8894-76e5b590f727" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502122 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nFirst, note that the incoming solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\n- The reactor initially contains $520\\,\\text{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Solvent enters the reactor at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $16t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the reactor.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the reactor at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the reactor.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the reactor after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n520+16t-20t=(520-4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of reactant in the reactor, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{130-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{5Q}{130-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{5}{130-t}Q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502130 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502130 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502130 #edb/ref 1071 "A $300\\,\\text{gal}$-capacity tank initially holds $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of water containing $45\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved contaminant. Clean water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed water flows out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Find the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502130 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f051584-9c22-538b-be73-a90ad4399f9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502130 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of water; clean water enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ and mixed water leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=180+6t-4t=(180+2t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{90+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{90+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{90+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(90+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $45\\,\\text{lb}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=45,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =45 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(90+0)^{2}} & =45 \\\\ A & =45(90)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{45(90)^{2}}{(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =45{(\\frac{90}{90+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $300\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=300$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 180+2{t}_{o} & =300 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =45{(\\frac{90}{90+60})}^{2} \\\\  & =45{(\\frac{90}{150})}^{2} \\\\  & =45{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =16.20\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502157 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502157 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502157 #edb/ref 1071 "A $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$-capacity water tank initially holds $7\\,200\\,\\text{mL}$ of water containing $210\\,\\text{g}$ of dissolved contaminant. Clean water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed water flows out at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ Find, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502157 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea65973e-51d0-5969-ad2d-3fe183a87ba6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502157 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $7\\,200\\,\\text{mL}$ of water; clean water enters at $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed water leaves at $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=7\\,200+90t-60t=30(240+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{240+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{240+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{240+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{240+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{240+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{240+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|240+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(240+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(240+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(240+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $210\\,\\text{g}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=210,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =210 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(240+0)^{2}} & =210 \\\\ A & =210(240)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{210(240)^{2}}{(240+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =210{(\\frac{240}{240+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=10\\,500$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 30(240+{t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 240+{t}_{o} & =350 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =110\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(110) & =210{(\\frac{240}{240+110})}^{2} \\\\  & =210{(\\frac{24}{35})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈98.74\\,\\text{g},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502158 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502158 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336490" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502158 #edb/ref 1071 "A processing tank initially holds $750\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $900\\,\\text{g}$ of dissolved sugar.\nA solution containing $18\\,\\text{g/L}$ of dissolved sugar is pumped into the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of sugar $Q$ in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502158 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ee7a7310-6a9a-5bb5-9fe8-376a138abe62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502158 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is the mass of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $18\\,\\text{g/L}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(18\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(18\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =108\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $750\\,\\text{L}$ of water.\n\n- A solution enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $6t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed water solution leaves the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $6t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n750+6t-6t=750\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the water solution at time $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{125}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =108-\\frac{Q}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{125}Q=108.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502161 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502161 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502161 #edb/ref 1071 "A tank initially holds $500\\,\\text{L}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $18\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt.\nA brine solution containing $0.12\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt per liter is poured into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of salt $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the brine begins to be added?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502161 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1d959d4-e84b-537b-9d31-22a9bf3bca3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502161 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a brine solution containing $0.12\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of salt enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.12\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.12\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =2.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $500\\,\\text{L}$ of brine.\n\n- Brine enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n500+20t-20t=500\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{25}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =2.4-\\frac{Q}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}Q=2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
